<p>
	I will try to make this article simple. To make it understandable not just=
 for the regular settlers here. On this site there is a lot of stuff you li=
ke a newcomer probably do not understand. They will instruct you to read a =
instructions which will make you even more confused at the beginning. Becau=
se it=E2=80=99s hard to imagine something behind the text or formulas. Most=
 of the time there is a talking about percentage of Monitor Distance ex.: 0=
% MM. What does it mean? I will show you on example with 100% Monitor dista=
nce =E2=80=93 vertical (<abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical"><abbr tit=
le=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical">MDV</abbr></abbr>). Converting from Window=
s to Game.
</p>

<p>
	In Windows desktop measure the distance traveled by the mouse, if the curs=
or moves across the entire screen, in the vertical axis. In other words, if=
 you move the cursor from the top edge of the screen to the bottom.
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_01.png.d5e8d85a34e570bb=
cd12f002b41f195b.png" data-fileid=3D"5900" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_01.png.d5e8d85a34e570bbcd12f002b41f195b.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5900" width=3D"634" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_01.png.d5e8d85a34e=
570bbcd12f002b41f195b.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"190.2" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	I measured 2,7 inches. Now if I want to reach the center from the top edge=
 of the screen. How much distance must mouse travel? A half right =3D&gt; 2=
,7/2 =3D 1,35 inches. So if you set a calculator here to match your game fo=
r 100% <abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical"><abbr title=3D"Monitor Dis=
tance Vertical">MDV</abbr></abbr>. It means that if you move your mouse ver=
tically about 1,35 inches. The cursor on screen in game will travel exact s=
ame monitor distance like in Windows. Ends at the edge of the screen.
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_02.png.a4184611d1c98829=
f21397448b8d145f.png" data-fileid=3D"5901" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_02.png.a4184611d1c98829f21397448b8d145f.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5901" width=3D"694" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_02.png.a4184611d1c=
98829f21397448b8d145f.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"159.62" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	Mouse distance 1,35 inches =3D A half of monitor distance in Windows and g=
ame. So at this mouse distance. We can say, that sensitivity is same for bo=
th Windows/Game.&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	However the sensitivity is same only for one point on the screen =3D one m=
ouse distance. Any other mouse distance lower or higher from 1,35 inches. W=
ill result in different monitor distance in Game. More or less. Because of =
2D/3D projection.<br>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_03.png.9aa441f85a167a69=
b6c3d3e2c5efad1e.png" data-fileid=3D"5902" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_03.png.9aa441f85a167a69b6c3d3e2c5efad1e.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5902" width=3D"239" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_03.png.9aa441f85a1=
67a69b6c3d3e2c5efad1e.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"150.57" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	I created a graph that allows me to see the projected path of the cursor o=
n the screen for several mouse distances. So you better understand what's g=
oing on. Graph is simulating one quarter of screen. We don't have to simula=
te the whole screen, because the rest will just be mirrored to this quarter=
.
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_04.png.507670866cccb755=
3fa596fd686c195c.png" data-fileid=3D"5903" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_04.png.507670866cccb7553fa596fd686c195c.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5903" width=3D"688" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_04.png.507670866cc=
cb7553fa596fd686c195c.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"192.64" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	Graph on right side is showing cursor path of our example 100% <abbr title=
=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical"><abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical">MD=
V</abbr></abbr>. Dots represents a cursor path. Green simulates a Windows. =
Red game. But as you can see, mouse sensitivity is same only for one point =
=3D one mouse distance =3D one monitor distance. All other mouse distances =
will result in a different monitor distance.
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_05.png.355a8b5f10e33ac2=
ed637eec4fb51a97.png" data-fileid=3D"5904" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_05.png.355a8b5f10e33ac2ed637eec4fb51a97.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5904" width=3D"542" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_05.png.355a8b5f10e=
33ac2ed637eec4fb51a97.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"254.74" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	Ideally, we want all pairs of points (Green/Red) to become one. But it=E2=
=80=99s mostly impossible to achieve it. Recently I found out how to find t=
he smallest difference between points for all mouse distances.
</p>

<p>
	<strong>Here comes the Jedi=E2=80=99s mouse trick:</strong><br>
	Set the monitor distance to 100% <abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical"=
><abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical">MDV</abbr></abbr> and note the 3=
60=C2=B0 distance =3D 13,1815.<br>
	Set the monitor distance to &nbsp; &nbsp; 0% <abbr title=3D"Monitor Distan=
ce Vertical"><abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical">MDV</abbr></abbr> an=
d note the 360=C2=B0 distance =3D 11,3097.<br>
	Manually adjust the sensitivity so that the 360=C2=B0 distance matches the=
 average of both values (13,1815 + 11,3097)/2 =3D 12,2456. By this you will=
 receive the smallest deviation for all points. I will demonstrate it on a =
Square graphs (ratio 1:1).
</p>

<p>
	This method looks easy. But it's practically 11 years of development behin=
d it (With pauses of course).&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_06.png.7ff011ad606f981e=
1f2cc4467a756973.png" data-fileid=3D"5907" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_06.png.7ff011ad606f981e1f2cc4467a756973.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5907" width=3D"702" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_06.png.7ff011ad606=
f981e1f2cc4467a756973.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"252.72" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	You can see the difference visually. But we can compare it also with numbe=
rs. I will use sum of absolute monitor distance deviations (SoD) for compar=
ing with 0% MM. We don=E2=80=99t care if the deviation is positive or negat=
ive. We just need to know absolute deviation. If the SoD value is lower, it=
 means that method has a lower deviation for all monitor distances. It will=
 be not to much precise. Because we are comparing just 9x points on screen.=
 But it=E2=80=99s enough to demonstrate the differences.
</p>

<p>
	Windows to Hipfire:
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_07.png.0378e925868b6693=
6c5fc50921234d77.png" data-fileid=3D"5908" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_07.png.0378e925868b66936c5fc50921234d77.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5908" width=3D"397" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_07.png.0378e925868=
b66936c5fc50921234d77.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"186.59" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	Windows to AWP Zoom 1:
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_08.png.3111da478a9dab9d=
fae0585a4f15e804.png" data-fileid=3D"5909" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_08.png.3111da478a9dab9dfae0585a4f15e804.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5909" width=3D"398" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_08.png.3111da478a9=
dab9dfae0585a4f15e804.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"187.06" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	Hipfire to AWP Zoom 1:
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"https://ww=
w.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_09.png.11a653262528d8b0=
9e5c98f506ee5995.png" data-fileid=3D"5910" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g80813=
"><img alt=3D"Web_09.png.11a653262528d8b09e5c98f506ee5995.png" class=3D"ips=
Image ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5910" width=3D"399" src=3D"https=
://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_09.png.11a65326252=
8d8b09e5c98f506ee5995.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"187.53" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox>
</p>

<p>
	If you do not want to adjust the distance for all points, but maybe only f=
or 40%. Repeat the same procedure, only use 40% instead of 100%. This give =
you the smallest deviation for all monitor distances in the range of 40% of=
 monitor screen.&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	There is many people who are trying to guide me to use 0% MM. No matter wh=
at. But they don't understand that they're taking me back 11 years to the e=
xact moment that led to the development of my Mouse sensitivity utility.
</p>

<p>
	I understand that 0% MM is mathematically correct. But when I leave the ga=
me back to Windows. I can't hit the icons, and it makes me mad <img alt=3D"=
:D" data-emoticon=3D"" height=3D"20" src=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.c=
om/uploads/emoticons/default_biggrin.png" title=3D":D" width=3D"20" loading=
=3D"lazy"> with 0% MM it=E2=80=99s even more noticeable. Because what we ar=
e doing in Windows most of the time? We are opening and closing windows. An=
d to close a window you have to move cursor all the way to upper right corn=
er of screen. And there is the biggest deviation for 0% MM. That=E2=80=99s =
why some people like to use 100% <abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Horizontal=
"><abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Horizontal">MDH</abbr></abbr>/V.&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	Jedi=E2=80=99s mouse trick is a compromise between tracking (0% MM) and fl=
icks (100% MM).&nbsp;
</p>


