&amp;controller=3Dprofile&amp;id=3D67588&amp;do=3Dhovercard">MacSquirrel_Je=
di</a>=E8=AA=AA:	</div>

	<div class=3D"ipsQuote_contents ipsClearfix" data-ipstruncate=3D"true" dat=
a-ipstruncate-expandtext=3D"=E5=B1=95=E9=96=8B" data-truncated=3D"true">
		<p>
			0% MM will be enough. No, it will not! <img alt=3D":D" data-emoticon=3D"=
" height=3D"20" src=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/emoticons/=
default_biggrin.png" title=3D":D" width=3D"20" loading=3D"lazy">
		</p>

		<p>
			I will try to make this article simple. To make it understandable not ju=
st for the regular settlers here. On this site there is a lot of stuff you =
like a newcomer probably do not understand. They will instruct you to read =
a instructions which will make you even more confused at the beginning. Bec=
ause it=E2=80=99s hard to imagine something behind the text or formulas. Mo=
st of the time there is a talking about percentage of Monitor Distance ex.:=
 0% MM. What does it mean? I will show you on example with 100% Monitor dis=
tance =E2=80=93 vertical (<abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical"><abbr t=
itle=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical">MDV</abbr></abbr>). Converting from Wind=
ows to Game.
		</p>

		<p>
			In Windows desktop measure the distance traveled by the mouse, if the cu=
rsor moves across the entire screen, in the vertical axis. In other words, =
if you move the cursor from the top edge of the screen to the bottom.
		</p>

		<p>
			<a href=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web=
_01.png.d5e8d85a34e570bbcd12f002b41f195b.png" title=3D"Enlarge image" data-=
fileid=3D"5900" data-fileext=3D"png" rel=3D"" class=3D"ipsAttachLink_block"=
><img alt=3D"Web_01.png.d5e8d85a34e570bbcd12f002b41f195b.png" class=3D"ipsI=
mage ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5900" width=3D"634" src=3D"https:=
//www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_01.png.d5e8d85a34e5=
70bbcd12f002b41f195b.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"190.2"></a>
		</p>

		<p>
			I measured 2,7 inches. Now if I want to reach the center from the top ed=
ge of the screen. How much distance must mouse travel? A half right =3D&gt;=
 2,7/2 =3D 1,35 inches. So if you set a calculator here to match your game =
for 100% <abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical"><abbr title=3D"Monitor D=
istance Vertical">MDV</abbr></abbr>. It means that if you move your mouse v=
ertically about 1,35 inches. The cursor on screen in game will travel exact=
 same monitor distance like in Windows. Ends at the edge of the screen.
		</p>

		<p>
			<a href=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web=
_02.png.a4184611d1c98829f21397448b8d145f.png" title=3D"Enlarge image" data-=
fileid=3D"5901" data-fileext=3D"png" rel=3D"" class=3D"ipsAttachLink_block"=
><img alt=3D"Web_02.png.a4184611d1c98829f21397448b8d145f.png" class=3D"ipsI=
mage ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5901" width=3D"694" src=3D"https:=
//www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_02.png.a4184611d1c9=
8829f21397448b8d145f.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"159.62"></a>
		</p>

		<p>
			Mouse distance 1,35 inches =3D A half of monitor distance in Windows and=
 game. So at this mouse distance. We can say, that sensitivity is same for =
both Windows/Game.&nbsp;
		</p>

		<p>
			However the sensitivity is same only for one point on the screen =3D one=
 mouse distance. Any other mouse distance lower or higher from 1,35 inches.=
 Will result in different monitor distance in Game. More or less. Because o=
f 2D/3D projection.<br>
			<a href=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web=
_03.png.9aa441f85a167a69b6c3d3e2c5efad1e.png" title=3D"=E7=8D=B2=E5=8F=96=
=E8=B3=87=E8=A8=8A..." data-fileid=3D"5902" data-fileext=3D"png" rel=3D"" c=
lass=3D"ipsAttachLink_inline" data-ipstooltip=3D"true"><img alt=3D"Web_03.p=
ng.9aa441f85a167a69b6c3d3e2c5efad1e.png" class=3D"ipsImage ipsImage_thumbna=
iled" data-fileid=3D"5902" width=3D"239" src=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivi=
ty.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_03.png.9aa441f85a167a69b6c3d3e2c5efad1e.=
png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"150.57"></a>
		</p>

		<p>
			I created a graph that allows me to see the projected path of the cursor=
 on the screen for several mouse distances. So you better understand what's=
 going on. Graph is simulating one quarter of screen. We don't have to simu=
late the whole screen, because the rest will just be mirrored to this quart=
er.
		</p>

		<p>
			<a href=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web=
_04.png.507670866cccb7553fa596fd686c195c.png" title=3D"Enlarge image" data-=
fileid=3D"5903" data-fileext=3D"png" rel=3D"" class=3D"ipsAttachLink_block"=
><img alt=3D"Web_04.png.507670866cccb7553fa596fd686c195c.png" class=3D"ipsI=
mage ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5903" width=3D"688" src=3D"https:=
//www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_04.png.507670866ccc=
b7553fa596fd686c195c.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"192.64"></a>
		</p>

		<p>
			Graph on right side is showing cursor path of our example 100% <abbr tit=
le=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical"><abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical">=
MDV</abbr></abbr>. Dots represents a cursor path. Green simulates a Windows=
. Red game. But as you can see, mouse sensitivity is same only for one poin=
t =3D one mouse distance =3D one monitor distance. All other mouse distance=
s will result in a different monitor distance.
		</p>

		<p>
			<a href=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web=
_05.png.355a8b5f10e33ac2ed637eec4fb51a97.png" title=3D"Enlarge image" data-=
fileid=3D"5904" data-fileext=3D"png" rel=3D"" class=3D"ipsAttachLink_block"=
><img alt=3D"Web_05.png.355a8b5f10e33ac2ed637eec4fb51a97.png" class=3D"ipsI=
mage ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5904" width=3D"542" src=3D"https:=
//www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_05.png.355a8b5f10e3=
3ac2ed637eec4fb51a97.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"254.74"></a>
		</p>

		<p>
			Ideally, we want all pairs of points (Green/Red) to become one. But it=
=E2=80=99s mostly impossible to achieve it. Recently I found out how to fin=
d the smallest difference between points for all mouse distances.
		</p>

		<p>
			<strong>Here comes the Jedi=E2=80=99s mouse trick:</strong><br>
			Set the monitor distance to 100% <abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertica=
l"><abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical">MDV</abbr></abbr> and note the=
 360=C2=B0 distance =3D 13,1815.<br>
			Set the monitor distance to &nbsp; &nbsp; 0% <abbr title=3D"Monitor Dist=
ance Vertical"><abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Vertical">MDV</abbr></abbr> =
and note the 360=C2=B0 distance =3D 11,3097.<br>
			Manually adjust the sensitivity so that the 360=C2=B0 distance matches t=
he average of both values (13,1815 + 11,3097)/2 =3D 12,2456. By this you wi=
ll receive the smallest deviation for all points. I will demonstrate it on =
a Square graphs (ratio 1:1).
		</p>

		<p>
			This method looks easy. But it's practically 11 years of development beh=
ind it (With pauses of course).&nbsp;
		</p>

		<p>
			<a href=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web=
_06.png.7ff011ad606f981e1f2cc4467a756973.png" title=3D"Enlarge image" data-=
fileid=3D"5907" data-fileext=3D"png" rel=3D"" class=3D"ipsAttachLink_block"=
><img alt=3D"Web_06.png.7ff011ad606f981e1f2cc4467a756973.png" class=3D"ipsI=
mage ipsImage_thumbnailed" data-fileid=3D"5907" width=3D"702" src=3D"https:=
//www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web_06.png.7ff011ad606f=
981e1f2cc4467a756973.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"252.72"></a>
		</p>

		<p>
			You can see the difference visually. But we can compare it also with num=
bers. I will use sum of absolute monitor distance deviations (SoD) for comp=
aring with 0% MM. We don=E2=80=99t care if the deviation is positive or neg=
ative. We just need to know absolute deviation. If the SoD value is lower, =
it means that method has a lower deviation for all monitor distances. It wi=
ll be not to much precise. Because we are comparing just 9x points on scree=
n. But it=E2=80=99s enough to demonstrate the differences.
		</p>

		<p>
			Windows to Hipfire:
		</p>

		<p>
			<a href=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/uploads/monthly_2021_11/Web=
_07.png.0378e925868b66936c5fc50921234d77.png" title=3D"Enlarge image" data-=
