d8b09e5c98f506ee5995.png" loading=3D"lazy" height=3D"187.53"></a>
		</p>

		<p>
			If you do not want to adjust the distance for all points, but maybe only=
 for 40%. Repeat the same procedure, only use 40% instead of 100%. This giv=
e you the smallest deviation for all monitor distances in the range of 40% =
of monitor screen.&nbsp;
		</p>

		<p>
			There is many people who are trying to guide me to use 0% MM. No matter =
what. But they don't understand that they're taking me back 11 years to the=
 exact moment that led to the development of my Mouse sensitivity utility.
		</p>

		<p>
			I understand that 0% MM is mathematically correct. But when I leave the =
game back to Windows. I can't hit the icons, and it makes me mad <img alt=
=3D":D" data-emoticon=3D"" height=3D"20" src=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivi=
ty.com/uploads/emoticons/default_biggrin.png" title=3D":D" width=3D"20" loa=
ding=3D"lazy"> with 0% MM it=E2=80=99s even more noticeable. Because what w=
e are doing in Windows most of the time? We are opening and closing windows=
. And to close a window you have to move cursor all the way to upper right =
corner of screen. And there is the biggest deviation for 0% MM. That=E2=80=
=99s why some people like to use 100% <abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Horiz=
ontal"><abbr title=3D"Monitor Distance Horizontal">MDH</abbr></abbr>/V.&nbs=
p;
		</p>

		<p>
			Jedi=E2=80=99s mouse trick is a compromise between tracking (0% MM) and =
flicks (100% MM).&nbsp;
		</p>
